ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

SECURITY LAB (security branch, based on v0.1.0)

This branch adds login, per-process UID/GID, and Unix-style owner/group/other
permissions. It is a small teaching implementation, not a production system.
The upstream acknowledgments and license above still apply.

Build and start:

    git clone -b security git@github.com:koukikitamura/xv6-riscv.git
    cd xv6-riscv
    make qemu

The build also needs Python 3. On macOS, the tools can be installed with:

    brew install riscv64-elf-gcc qemu clang-format

Log in with one of the fixed lab accounts. Each password equals the username:

    user     UID    group    GID    initial directory
    root       0    root       0   /
    alice   1000    users    100   /alice
    bob     1001    users    100   /bob
    carol   1002    guests   200   /carol

Passwords are not echoed. Ctrl-D at an empty shell prompt logs out; Ctrl-a x
exits QEMU. Bare commands run from /; use ./name to run a local program.
The four account passwords are public demonstration credentials.

As alice:

    ls memo.txt
    cat memo.txt
    chmod 640 memo.txt

The first ls displays "-rw------- alice users memo.txt". After chmod, bob can
read /alice/memo.txt because he belongs to users, but cannot overwrite it.
Carol cannot read it. Only root may change its owner or group:

    chown bob:guests /alice/memo.txt
    chmod 600 /alice/memo.txt
    ls /alice/memo.txt

chmod accepts three or four octal digits (000 through 0777). chown accepts a
username or user:group; without :group it uses that user's primary group.
ls shows the mode, owner, group and filename. Unknown IDs are shown numerically.
There are no account creation, password change, or supplementary-group tools.

PERMISSION RULES

A process has one UID and one primary GID. fork copies them; exec preserves them.
setidentity changes both atomically and is allowed only while UID is zero.
There are no saved IDs or setuid/setgid executables: dropping root is permanent.

For non-root processes, exactly one permission class applies: owner if UID
matches, otherwise group if GID matches, otherwise other. A failed owner or
group check does not fall back to the other bits. Root bypasses permission
checks, except that executing a regular file still requires some execute bit.

Opening a file checks read/write permission before truncating it. O_TRUNC also
requires a write access mode. exec requires execute permission, not read
permission. Directories need execute permission for pathname traversal/chdir,
read permission for listing names, and write plus execute permission for
creating/removing entries. Deletion depends on the parent, not the file's mode.
New files default to 0644, directories to 0755, devices to 0600; there is no
umask. stat can inspect an unreadable file when its path is searchable.
Already-open descriptors keep their access rights after chmod or identity
changes. Pipes continue to work through inherited descriptors.

Only root can create devices or change file ownership. Owners and root may
chmod. Hard links require ownership of the source (or root) and write/search
permission on the destination directory. kill is restricted to the same UID
or root, and init cannot be killed. IDs range from 0 to 65535.

The console is a single shared terminal, not a full terminal/session system.
Only root can configure its input owner and echo. During authentication input
belongs to root; after login it belongs to that user's UID. Changing owners
flushes pending input and wakes blocked readers, which recheck ownership.
init restores input ownership and echo when the login/shell process exits.
Background processes may survive logout; console output remains shared.

PASSWORD STORAGE

/passwd and /group hold fixed-size public account records. /shadow is owned by
root with mode 0600 and contains only UID, iteration count, salt and derived
hash. Record layouts are in user/accounts.h; integers are little-endian.

There is one password format: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 with a random 16-byte salt,
100,000 iterations and a 32-byte result. HMAC and SHA-256 are internal parts of
this format, not selectable alternatives. tools/mkaccounts.py obtains salts
from the host's OS random source and computes the initial hashes with hashlib.
user/password.c performs authentication inside xv6. The iteration count is a
fixed teaching/performance choice, not a production password-policy claim.
Input is limited to 63 bytes; overlong input is drained and rejected. Login
closes the credential file and clears password/hash buffers before executing
the user's shell. Hash comparison examines all bytes. Failed logins incur a
short delay; there is no persistent account lockout or remote authentication.

The algorithm definitions are RFC 8018 section 5.2 and RFC 6234. Host tests
compare the C implementation against Python hashlib and HMAC across padding
boundaries, long HMAC keys and multiple iteration counts.

DISK FORMAT

UID, GID and mode are persistent inode fields. dinode is 128 bytes and FSMAGIC
is changed, so an image from another branch is rejected. Rebuild the image
when switching branches. make clean && make qemu resets all lab files and
accounts. Rebuilding fs.img also resets changes and generates new salts.
Never rebuild the image while another QEMU instance is using it.

TESTS

    python3 tools/test_password.py
    python3 tools/test_security.py
    python3 tools/test_security.py --skip-usertests --cpus 1
    ./test-xv6.py usertests
    ./test-xv6.py crash
    make fmt

The security runner uses a temporary disk copy, tests incorrect and overlong
passwords, command behavior, the kernel permission matrix, credential
inheritance, forbidden privilege changes, directory permissions, execute-only
files, truncation, existing descriptors and terminal ownership. It reboots to
check ownership/mode persistence. By default it also runs usertests -q; --full
runs all usertests instead. Transcripts are saved under lab/.

The existing test-xv6.py logs in as root automatically. It resets fs.img for
most tests. Crash tests kill the entire make/QEMU process group, which works
on both Linux and macOS. Kernel tests can also be run as root from the shell:

    securitytests
    usertests

READING ORDER

1. kernel/proc.h, proc.c, sysproc.c: identity and process ownership.
2. kernel/fs.h, file.h, stat.h and mkfs/mkfs.c: persistent metadata and accounts.
3. kernel/fs.c: iaccess and pathname traversal.
4. kernel/sysfile.c and exec.c: permission checks at operation boundaries.
5. user/init.c, login.c, accounts.c and kernel/console.c: authentication flow.
6. user/password.c: SHA-256, HMAC and the single-block PBKDF2 derivation.
7. user/chmod.c, chown.c, ls.c and securitytests.c: commands and verification.
